WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.674
Regression models how a response tends to vary with an explanatory input

00:00:05.674 --> 00:00:13.145
A fitted line predicts a response for each input, while observations can sit above or below it

00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:17.100
Those vertical differences are residuals, not necessarily recording errors.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:26.385
For these points, the line one half plus one point five x predicts one half, two, and three point five

00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:31.975
Subtracting predictions from observed responses gives residuals one half, negative one, and one half

00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:36.367
Their signs record which side of the line each point occupies.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:44.606
Ordinary least squares chooses the intercept and slope that minimize the sum of squared residuals

00:00:44.606 --> 00:00:50.157
Squaring prevents positive and negative residuals from cancelling and emphasizes larger deviations

00:00:50.157 --> 00:00:56.633
For these three points, this line achieves the minimum total of one point five.

00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:03.436
Predicting at five extends beyond the observed input range of zero to two

00:01:03.436 --> 00:01:09.361
The line can produce that number without evidence that the relationship remains linear there

00:01:09.361 --> 00:01:16.133
A fitted association also does not establish that changing the input causes the response to change.
